1
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,420
I generally try to do what I can from memory,

2
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:15,560
but I'm going to do this very quickly.

3
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:20,620
We started out with John McMillan, who was known

4
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:24,140
as the apostle of Presbyterianism in

5
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:27,060
the West, and who was the founder of Bethel

6
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:31,540
and Lebanon Churches, beginning with the notes

7
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:35,060
in his journal, November the 5th, 1776, at the

8
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:35,920
Miller Homestead.

9
00:00:36,020 --> 00:00:39,980
So actually, our church began in the home of an

10
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:41,580
early pioneer family.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:48,020
The congregation divided into two groups, East

12
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:53,180
and West Peters Creek Churches, and

13
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:56,660
John McMillan came back and acted as pastor

14
00:00:56,660 --> 00:00:59,960
of Hill Church and Pigeon Creek Churches

15
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:04,420
and was founder of Canonsburg Academy and

16
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,200
Jefferson College

17
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:08,420
and was also a trustee of Washington College.

18
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:13,120
We know that he was a close friend of our first

19
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:15,520
pastor, Reverend John Clark,

20
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:20,020
and we know also that he was executor of John

21
00:01:20,020 --> 00:01:20,860
Clark's will.

22
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:25,760
Then we spent some time on the background of

23
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,440
John Clark, born in New Jersey, and we hope

24
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:28,680
maybe

25
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:31,220
someday your mother will help us find out where,

26
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:35,740
because we don't know as yet, who served a

27
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:36,060
number

28
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:39,520
of churches in New York, New Jersey, Delaware,

29
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:44,260
and Pennsylvania, and was serving a Bethel

30
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:44,500
church

31
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:51,340
in Maryland before he came here. His greatest

32
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:53,820
claim to fame I think in terms

33
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:57,570
of American history is his participation in the

34
00:01:57,570 --> 00:02:00,490
Whiskey Rebellion, when he attempted to prevent

35
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:03,090
the farmers from becoming violent in their

36
00:02:03,090 --> 00:02:05,410
attack against the tax collector.

37
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:10,030
But in terms of Presbyterian history, I think

38
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:13,430
his greatest contribution was the fact that he

39
00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:17,450
left his land, which is where the senior high

40
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:20,070
school is today, in a part of Coverdale that was

41
00:02:20,070 --> 00:02:21,130
called Plain Truth.

42
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:25,290
He turned that over upon the death of his wife,

43
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,210
Margaret, to Jefferson College with the

44
00:02:28,210 --> 00:02:31,090
provision that when the land was sold, that

45
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:35,490
money would go to help maintain the college and

46
00:02:35,490 --> 00:02:40,610
also provide a scholarship fund for prospective

47
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:41,230
ministers.

48
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:45,670
Young and pious, needy, intelligent, energetic

49
00:02:45,670 --> 00:02:49,570
men who would help to further the teaching of

50
00:02:49,570 --> 00:02:51,350
the faith.

51
00:02:56,330 --> 00:02:59,490
There were revivals that took place during his

52
00:02:59,490 --> 00:03:00,330
pastorate.

53
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:05,970
And we know through Presbyterian history that

54
00:03:05,970 --> 00:03:12,870
his slave, this is a real jar to us, that he

55
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:15,150
brought slaves with him from Maryland.

56
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:19,810
His slave Dido had a beautiful voice and was

57
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:22,330
known for her singing during the revival period.

58
00:03:22,330 --> 00:03:28,650
We know that by the will of John Clark that

59
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:31,890
his slave Dido was set free. We don't

60
00:03:31,890 --> 00:03:35,610
really know what happened to Dave, the male

61
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:41,510
slave. He is listed in the inventory of John

62
00:03:41,510 --> 00:03:44,630
Clark, and so this is another question mark that

63
00:03:44,630 --> 00:03:47,770
we have yet to research. So this is the

64
00:03:47,770 --> 00:03:51,610
period, period, really, of the frontier of our

65
00:03:51,610 --> 00:03:54,350
church and this part of Pennsylvania.

66
00:03:55,900 --> 00:04:00,060
It's ironic that in the year 1794, which was the

67
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:03,240
year of the Whiskey Rebellion, this was also the

68
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:06,380
year that Mad Anthony Wayne broke the power of

69
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:10,440
the Indians in the Ohio Territory, and they were

70
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:15,360
pushed farther west so that the land west of the

71
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:17,400
Ohio became open for settlement.

72
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:20,220
And then with the purchase, the Louisiana

73
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:24,640
Purchase, during Thomas Jefferson's

74
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,720
administration, New Orleans and Louisiana became

75
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,840
a part of this country.

76
00:04:32,020 --> 00:04:36,700
And all of the reasons why the Whiskey Rebellion

77
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:39,660
occurred, their lack of trade and their

78
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:42,480
dependence upon whiskey as a cash crop, all of

79
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,520
that was just completely wiped out.

80
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:49,560
And Pittsburgh became known as the gateway to

81
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:49,960
the West.

82
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:55,200
And you had the farmers in this area continued

83
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:55,940
farming,

84
00:04:56,040 --> 00:05:01,500
but mainly to provide hay and straw and farm

85
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:05,500
commodities for the people in the area.

86
00:05:05,520 --> 00:05:07,400
But their greatest market was in Pittsburgh.

87
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:11,900
And descendants of the earliest settlers

88
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:14,440
who were involved in the Whiskey Rebellion

89
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:16,920
became gradually caught up

90
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,920
in what was called the Temperance Movement

91
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:23,460
and very soon you found that the very families

92
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:26,520
that had been involved at one time

93
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:28,160
in the Whiskey Rebellion were now

94
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:31,260
very much opposed to the use of alcoholic

95
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:32,540
beverages

96
00:05:32,540 --> 00:05:35,300
it's a very interesting development

97
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:36,780
in our history

98
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:40,500
on the death of John Clark

99
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:43,200
came our second pastor

100
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:44,120
William Woods

101
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:47,880
who was born in Lancaster County

102
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:50,940
he had teachers who were

103
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:54,400
who had trained John McMillan

104
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,040
And he came into this area and bought up the

105
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:05,280
farm from Jefferson College that had belonged to

106
00:06:05,280 --> 00:06:05,900
John Clark.

107
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:11,140
So he, in essence, lived in the home of John

108
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:11,560
Clark.

109
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:14,940
And if you want a picture about where it is, go

110
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:18,160
down Church Road and you see that parking area

111
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,580
below where the tennis courts are.

112
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:24,740
It was about in that area that the house that

113
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,940
was occupied by John Clark and later by William

114
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:29,060
Woods stood.

115
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:38,100
There was a revival in 1802 that took place in

116
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,180
the early part of his pastorate.

117
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:44,760
This was a time when many, many more people

118
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:48,880
became residents of this area.

119
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:53,820
And the original land plantations were divided

120
00:06:53,820 --> 00:06:54,840
up and sold.

121
00:06:55,440 --> 00:07:00,180
And as a result of that, you found that Bethel

122
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:04,540
began to lose its members that, like James

123
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:07,220
Dinsmore, had many, many miles to travel.

124
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,600
And so we had break-offs.

125
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:15,000
Bethany, which is in Bridgeville, was formed in

126
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:15,760
1814.

127
00:07:15,760 --> 00:07:21,260
And Center Church in 1828, Mount Pisgah, which

128
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:26,320
is in Green Tree in 1830, and Concord, which is

129
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:28,460
in Carrick, and I just did a program for them

130
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:30,440
last week, and they were so amazed when I

131
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:30,680
brought

132
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:35,700
the history book and read to them about Bethel's

133
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:42,240
being the mother of their church. Many of the

134
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:44,360
people were aware of this, but many new

135
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:45,340
members were not.

136
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:47,080
And this was something that they were very

137
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:47,820
interested in.

138
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:53,160
Another thing that shows that the trend was

139
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:53,520
beginning

140
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:55,220
to change was that the old

141
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:58,700
The log meeting house that you see here which

142
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:01,180
was located across the road where the parking

143
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:06,680
area is was torn down and a second structure was

144
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:12,080
built and it was our first brick meeting house

145
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:16,560
and it stood approximately where this part of

146
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:18,200
the church is now.

147
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:22,300
In order to build that, there was several other

148
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:26,420
land purchases, and over on the desk there, when

149
00:08:26,420 --> 00:08:29,320
you have time, you'll see there is the actual

150
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:34,860
sale of the land by Thomas and Elizabeth Tidball

151
00:08:34,860 --> 00:08:39,620
to the trustees of the church took place.

152
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:44,680
And this brings up one of the beginnings of

153
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:50,240
a great contention as to where the church was

154
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,620
going to be built on the land. And I thought I'd

155
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:57,520
read this to you. The story has come down to us

156
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:00,600
through the years of a great argument as to

157
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:05,180
where the new church should stand. Which hill?

158
00:09:05,820 --> 00:09:06,620
This one

159
00:09:06,620 --> 00:09:11,060
or the one at the top of the cemetery and the

160
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:13,840
question I guess in their minds was which was

161
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,180
the highest hill. I don't really know why it

162
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:19,580
became such a terrible issue but it did.

163
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:23,580
Two sites were favored by opposing parties and

164
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:26,720
feeling ran high as neither faction was willing

165
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:30,760
to yield. At last one day a congregational

166
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,660
meeting was being held at the log meeting house

167
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:37,560
and one of the leaders said, all them that favor

168
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:40,740
that hill, indicating the present site,

169
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:45,160
stay where you are, and all them that favor the

170
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:48,000
other hill, where the McCormick house now

171
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:48,340
stands,

172
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:51,020
and I'll remember that house is probably gone by

173
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:53,880
now, above the cemetery, follow me.

174
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:57,780
Being on his horse, he started to ride toward

175
00:09:57,780 --> 00:09:59,160
the site that he favored.

176
00:09:59,650 --> 00:10:02,910
But the people stayed where they were, thus

177
00:10:02,910 --> 00:10:06,250
casting their vote for the present site as the

178
00:10:06,250 --> 00:10:07,990
location of the new building.

179
00:10:08,830 --> 00:10:14,110
And this began an argument, and again, as I say,

180
00:10:14,130 --> 00:10:16,530
it seems almost incredible that it should become

181
00:10:16,530 --> 00:10:20,570
a dividing factor among the members of the

182
00:10:20,570 --> 00:10:21,950
congregation, but it was.

183
00:10:21,950 --> 00:10:25,870
And it was one of the reasons why Mr. Woods,

184
00:10:26,150 --> 00:10:33,950
Reverend Woods, requested that he be able to

185
00:10:33,950 --> 00:10:37,150
withdraw as pastor of Bethel Church.

186
00:10:41,730 --> 00:10:48,810
He died shortly after George Marshall came as

187
00:10:48,810 --> 00:10:49,830
our new pastor.

188
00:10:52,990 --> 00:10:58,150
Now, George Marshall was born near Milton in

189
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:03,510
Union County, and he was the oldest of

190
00:11:03,510 --> 00:11:04,230
five children.

191
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:08,210
His father died when he was very, very young,

192
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:12,730
and so he was raised by his mother, who was

193
00:11:12,730 --> 00:11:16,370
a very pious woman, and she made sure that he

194
00:11:16,370 --> 00:11:18,650
got the best education possible.

195
00:11:18,650 --> 00:11:22,890
He studied under David Kirkpatrick at Milton

196
00:11:22,890 --> 00:11:26,610
Academy and attended Dickinson College, which I

197
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:27,570
think is in Carlisle.

198
00:11:29,970 --> 00:11:35,350
Then in 1828, he entered Jefferson College.

199
00:11:36,510 --> 00:11:40,010
And at the time that he attended Jefferson

200
00:11:40,010 --> 00:11:44,770
College, he earned his tuition by acting as a

201
00:11:44,770 --> 00:11:47,210
teacher at Cross Creek Academy.

202
00:11:47,210 --> 00:11:51,090
Now these are all things important for us to

203
00:11:51,090 --> 00:11:53,210
know because it helps us to understand

204
00:11:53,210 --> 00:11:56,330
the things that were important to him.

205
00:11:56,450 --> 00:11:56,950
Education.

206
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:02,760
And teaching and providing schooling for

207
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:07,280
promising young people was very much a part of

208
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:13,060
his life and it strikes me that the work of John

209
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:18,560
McMillan and John Clark who gave so much of

210
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:20,980
their time and their money and their energy to

211
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:25,560
the forming of Jefferson College was paying off

212
00:12:25,560 --> 00:12:27,340
because here was a young man

213
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:30,840
who came from the central part of the state, who

214
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:34,220
was taking advantage of the education which

215
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:38,140
they, through their wisdom, provided in those

216
00:12:38,140 --> 00:12:38,900
early years.

217
00:12:39,940 --> 00:12:42,820
And during the time that he was a teacher at

218
00:12:42,820 --> 00:12:47,000
Cross Creek Academy, he studied under the, I

219
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,760
believe he was the second or third pastor of

220
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:53,840
Cross Creek Presbyterian Church.

221
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:59,440
His name was Dr. John Stockton, and his house is

222
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:02,480
still in existence in Cross Creek.

223
00:13:06,620 --> 00:13:10,540
George Marshall married a young girl by the name

224
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:15,020
of Mary Lee, whose father, Hugh Lee, was an

225
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:18,100
elder in the Cross Creek Presbyterian Church.

226
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:21,500
So there were very close ties, family ties,

227
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:26,360
between the Marshalls and members of Cross

228
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:26,780
Creek.

229
00:13:28,340 --> 00:13:31,500
Shortly after he graduated from Jefferson

230
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:36,160
College, he was called to preach at Bethel.

231
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:39,420
And the description of him was rather

232
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:40,080
interesting.

233
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:46,780
Before Reverend William Woods, our second

234
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:49,700
pastor, had gone home, his successor appeared on

235
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:50,240
the field.

236
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:53,500
Reverend George Marshall, a licentiate of

237
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:55,080
Washington Presbytery,

238
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:57,940
Came to Bethel in the providence of God and

239
00:13:57,940 --> 00:14:02,760
preached his first sermon July 11, 1832. One of

240
00:14:02,760 --> 00:14:04,280
his hearers that day said,

241
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:08,100
He stood pale and thin like a ghost, but his

242
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:11,320
voice and gestures were powerful. His prayers

243
00:14:11,320 --> 00:14:12,900
were better than his preaching.

244
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:16,920
He was young but spoke with authority. We were

245
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:20,400
taken by storm and we determined to have him.

246
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:24,080
For a time, he acted as stated supply and

247
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:27,080
preached so acceptably that he received a

248
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:28,120
unanimous call.

249
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:34,680
And so he became pastor of, third pastor of

250
00:14:34,680 --> 00:14:36,340
Bethel Presbyterian Church.

251
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:41,340
And when he came in to this area, he purchased a

252
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:46,120
tract of land from William Tidball, which is

253
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:47,220
Oakhurst.

254
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:52,040
And Marshall Road is the road that is named for

255
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:55,700
George Marshall and his old farmhouse is now one

256
00:14:55,700 --> 00:14:58,900
of the most beautiful homes in Oakhurst it's

257
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:02,060
where the Doms live today it looks like a big

258
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:02,600
plantation

259
00:15:02,600 --> 00:15:07,960
house with a big weeping willow trees and if you

260
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:14,280
want to get a shock this is a picture here this

261
00:15:14,280 --> 00:15:17,160
is a picture of the farmhouse as it looked

262
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:20,700
before it was bought by the Hicks family.

263
00:15:21,580 --> 00:15:26,160
And, you know, so much of our history is gone

264
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:30,780
because Americans are notorious for tearing down

265
00:15:30,780 --> 00:15:33,920
and replacing something without really

266
00:15:33,920 --> 00:15:36,500
considering its historical value.

267
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:40,360
The Hicks family were an exception to that.

268
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:44,140
They took that old farmhouse and remodeled it

269
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:48,100
and added parts to it until today it's one of

